h colonial oppression, but we will also study their response as agents of change in providing alternative paradigms and insights to humanity in the third millennium.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": null, "catalogEnrollmentPriority": "", "catalogLang": null, "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": null, "catalogAttribute": null, "catalogWhenOffered": null, "catalogComments": null, "catalogPrereqCoreq": null, "catalogPrereq": "", "catalogCoreq": "", "catalogFee": null, "catalogSatisfiesReq": null, "catalogPermission": null, "catalogCourseSubfield": null, "catalogOutcomes": ["To gain perspective of contemporary issues in Indigenous Studies with a historical sense that not only conveys the pastness of the past but its presence and relevance for the future.", "To examine current issues in Indigenous Studies that are important to communities.", "To apply an interdisciplinary lens in understanding indigenous sociocultural and ecological issues.", "To appreciate the complex interconnectivity between the ecological and the sociocultural.", "To comprehend that policy actions informed by cultural systems manifest themselves in social structures that rely on ecological foundations.", "To situate Indigenous Studies within a humanistic framework of knowledge generation.", "To illustrate the relevance and contribution of Indigenous Studies to broader issues of humanity in the 21st Century.", "To discern a methodology of hope based on indigenous experience."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "catalogName": null, "catalogNextOffered": null, "lastTermsOffered": "2026SP, 2025SP, 2024SP, 2023SP", "catalogCredits": null, "catalogGradeOption": null, "catalogLocation": null, "catalogCrosslisted": null, "catalogCrosslistings": null, "catalogComeets": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2923, "crseId": 352722, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "2600", "titleShort": "Intro to Native American Lits", "titleLong": "Introduction to Native American Literature", "crseA